\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.3.1}{\ignorespaces Radial Velocities of HD~143811~A and B. Widely separated epochs are where the two sets of lines are distinct from each other, which corresponds to $\Delta RV \gtrsim 20 km/s$}}{14}{table.caption.7}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.3.2}{\ignorespaces Properties of HD~143811~AB. Adopted parameters are indicated with a Y. We use an age for the system of $13 \pm 4$ Myr based on the uncertain Upper Sco or UCL membership, though the posterior from the SED fit favors somewhat older ages. Temperature and metallicity are adopted from the SED fit rather than the spectral fit.}}{28}{table.caption.11}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The age posteriors for HIP 117410 for both periods and the combined posteriors are listed. We adopt the combined age posterior for this system.}}{65}{table.caption.25}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces The estimated parameters of the three eclipsing binaries in our sample.}}{66}{table.caption.27}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Our complete sample of accelerating stars with age posteriors and age tracers used. Ages come from lithium (Li), lithium upper limits (Li UL), calcium emission (R'HK), rotation periods (Gyro), and CMD positions (CMD).}}{71}{table.3.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.1}{\ignorespaces We present our lithium equivalent width measurements or upper limits for each star and its corresponding \texttt {BAFFLES} {\color {black} median age and confidence intervals} where appropriate. For stars with $B-V$ outside the \texttt {BAFFLES} range, we do not derive age posteriors.}}{98}{table.A.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.2}{\ignorespaces Each target star and its adopted $B-V$, measured S index and measured $log(R^{'}_{HK})$ are listed. $R^{'}_{HK}$ is only calibrated as an age metric for F5-K2 stars for which report a median age, $68\%$ and $98\%$ confidence intervals.}}{106}{table.A.2}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.3}{\ignorespaces Rotation period measurements for 23 of our 166 accelerating stars. An additional 106 stars have a TESS light curve but display either no variability signal or a signal that is not due to rotation, and 37 stars do not have a TESS light curve. {\color {black} Median ages and $68\%$ and $95\%$ confidence intervals} are given for stars with a rotation period measurement that are also within the \texttt {gyro-interp} $T_{eff}$ range.}}{114}{table.A.3}%
